---
// Server-rendered for the same reason as the rest of /kb: entries change
// between deploys.
export const prerender = false;

import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/kb.css';

const KB_TOOLS = [
  { name: 'search_knowledge', auth: 'public', text: 'Full-text search over every entry. Prefix matching, so `migra` finds `migration`.' },
  { name: 'get_knowledge', auth: 'public', text: 'One entry in full (problem and solution markdown) by id or slug.' },
  { name: 'list_knowledge', auth: 'public', text: 'Newest entries first, without bodies.' },
  { name: 'create_knowledge', auth: 'ack_ key', text: 'Record a problem and solution pair from a session.' },
  { name: 'update_knowledge', auth: 'ack_ key', text: 'Patch an entry this key authored.' },
];
---

<Base
  site="lab"
  title="The agent notes over MCP — M. Ali Petek's lab"
  description="How my own agents read and write their notes over MCP at https://malipetek.dev/mcp. Open to read, but uncurated: I would not connect your agent to it."
  current="/kb"
>
  <main class="wrap kb">
    <header class="kb-head">
      <p class="kb-kicker">Model Context Protocol</p>
      <h1>The agent notes over MCP.</h1>
      <p class="kb-lede">
        My own agents read and write <a href="/kb">their notes</a> through
        <a href="/mcp"><code>https://malipetek.dev/mcp</code></a> (streamable HTTP, protocol
        <code>2025-06-18</code>). Reading is open to anyone, but the notes are uncurated findings
        from my projects, so I would not connect your agent to them. If you want to anyway, this
        page shows how. Writing needs an <code>ack_</code> key, which only my agents have. The
        client portal has its own server at <a href="https://malipetek.dev/mcp">malipetek.dev/mcp</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="kb-block">
      <h2>Connect</h2>
      <div class="kb-prose">
        <p>
          In Claude: <strong>Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector</strong>. In
          ChatGPT: turn on <strong>Settings → Connectors → Advanced → Developer mode</strong>,
          then add a connector. In Cursor, <code>mcp.json</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>&#123; "mcpServers": &#123; "malipetek-kb": &#123; "url": "https://malipetek.dev/mcp" &#125; &#125; &#125;</code></pre>
        <p>No client at all:</p>
        <pre><code>curl -s https://malipetek.dev/mcp -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '&#123;"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/list"&#125;'</code></pre>
        <p>
          REST works too, without MCP:
        </p>
        <pre><code>curl https://malipetek.dev/kb/entries?q=fts5
curl https://malipetek.dev/kb/&lt;slug&gt;.json</code></pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="kb-block">
      <h2>Onboarding: what an agent needs, and what it does not</h2>
      <div class="kb-prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Reading needs nothing.</strong> <code>search_knowledge</code>,
          <code>get_knowledge</code>, <code>list_knowledge</code>, every
          <code>kb://entry/…</code> resource and the whole REST API are open. No key, no
          sign-up, no handshake.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Writing needs a key Ali mints.</strong> That is the point: open to read,
          authored only by us. There is deliberately no self-serve route.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Ali signs in at <a href="https://malipetek.dev/portal">malipetek.dev/portal</a> and opens <a href="/kb/new">/kb/new</a>.</li>
          <li>He mints a key named after the agent that will hold it (<code>claude-code</code>, <code>cursor</code>…).</li>
          <li>The agent sends it as <code>Authorization: Bearer ack_…</code>. It is stored only as a SHA-256 hash and shown once.</li>
          <li>Every use updates <code>last_used_at</code>. Revoking is one click, so a leaked key is visible and short-lived.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="kb-block">
      <h2>Knowledgebase tools</h2>
      <ul class="kb-tool-list">
        {KB_TOOLS.map((tool) => (
          <li>
            <code>{tool.name}</code>
            <span class="kb-tool-auth">{tool.auth}</span>
            <span class="kb-tool-text">{tool.text}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="kb-entry-foot">
        Mint an <code>ack_</code> key at <a href="/kb/new"><code>/kb/new</code></a> (owner
        only) and pass it as <code>Authorization: Bearer ack_…</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="kb-block">
      <h2>Resources and prompts</h2>
      <div class="kb-prose">
        <p>
          Tools are for calling; <strong>resources</strong> are for attaching. Every entry is
          also a resource at <code>kb://entry/&lt;slug&gt;</code> (<code>text/markdown</code>),
          so a client can pin the relevant entry into context instead of asking the model to
          fetch it. <code>resources/list</code> pages at 50 with a cursor, and
          <code>kb://entry/&#123;slug&#125;</code> is advertised as a template.
        </p>
        <p>
          One <strong>prompt</strong>, <code>record_learning</code>, takes a topic and an
          outcome and returns the instructions for turning the session that just ended into
          an entry: search first so it updates rather than duplicates, and pick the status
          honestly.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every tool carries MCP annotations, so a connector can tell reading from writing
          before it asks. <code>readOnlyHint</code> is true for search and listing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="kb-block">
      <h2>WebMCP: the same knowledge, inside the page</h2>
      <div class="kb-prose">
        <p>
          A backend MCP server is something the agent's platform reaches.
          <strong>WebMCP</strong> (<code>document.modelContext</code>) is the other
          direction: the page registers tools that reuse its own code and keep the visible
          UI in sync with what the agent just did.
        </p>
        <p>
          This site registers two, feature-detected. A browser without the API loads the
          module and does nothing at all.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <code>search_knowledge(query, status?)</code> returns entries, and updates the
            visible results when the visitor is on <a href="/kb">/kb</a>.
          </li>
          <li><code>open_knowledge_entry(slug)</code> opens the entry in the visitor's own browser.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The contracting site has its own <code>open_intercom</code> tool, registered only
          on pages that have an intercom. Every registered tool spends context window and
          invites tool confusion, so the lists stay short.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="kb-block">
      <h2>What this is not</h2>
      <div class="kb-prose">
        <p>
          This endpoint has no client portal tools. If you are a client of Ali's and want
          your assistant to file or follow a request, use
          <a href="https://malipetek.dev/agents">malipetek.dev/agents</a> instead. The two
          servers are separate on purpose: a summary of one should not describe the other.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
